            2. DIY Cost Breakdown: Coatings and Crack Injection
          </h2>
          <p>
            DIY basement waterproofing falls into two categories, and it's important to understand what each one actually does before you buy anything. A masonry waterproof coating (Drylok, RadonSeal, and similar silicate or elastomeric products) seals the interior face of the concrete wall against minor dampness and efflorescence. A crack injection kit seals an active structural crack that's letting water through a specific point. Neither one addresses bulk water intrusion from hydrostatic pressure — for that, see our full guide on <a href="/how-to-waterproof-a-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-medium">how to waterproof a basement</a> and our tested picks for <a href="/best-basement-waterproofing-supplies/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-medium">the best basement waterproofing supplies</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-6">DIY Masonry Coating — Average Basement (~150 ln ft / ~1,100 sq ft wall)</p>
