          <p>
            Be honest with yourself about what this buys you. Masonry coatings work well against minor dampness, efflorescence, and slow seepage through porous concrete block. They do not stop active water intrusion under hydrostatic pressure — if water is pushing through the wall or up through the floor-wall joint with any force, a surface coating will bubble and delaminate within a season or two. This is the single most common DIY waterproofing mistake we see: homeowners paint over a symptom of a drainage problem and are surprised when the coating fails during the next heavy rain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Crack Injection: A Different Tool for a Different Problem</h3>
          <p>
            If your wet basement traces to one or two specific cracks rather than general wall dampness, crack injection is usually the better and cheaper fix. A two-part polyurethane or epoxy injection kit costs $50–$130 and treats a single crack up to about 4–6 feet long, injecting sealant directly into the crack under low pressure so it cures within the concrete itself rather than sitting on the surface. Polyurethane is the better choice for actively leaking cracks since it expands on contact with water; epoxy is stronger and better suited to structural cracks that need to be bonded, not just sealed. An average basement with 2–4 cracks runs $150–$500 in materials. For a deeper look at diagnosing and repairing different crack types, see our dedicated guide to <a href="/basement-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-medium">basement crack repair</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Professional crack injection costs $250–$600 per crack, which sounds steep next to a $50–$130 DIY kit, but the labor is genuinely fast (30–60 minutes per crack) and the markup reflects specialized injection ports and pumps, not raw material cost. For a single, accessible, non-structural crack, DIY injection has a high success rate. For a crack wider than 1/4 inch, a stair-step crack, or one accompanied by wall bowing, get a structural evaluation before injecting anything — you may be sealing a symptom of a bigger foundation problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 3: Interior vs Exterior -->
