          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Interior vs. Exterior: Why the Price Gap Is So Large
          </h2>
          <p>
            This is the question that matters most once you're past the DIY-scale fixes. Exterior waterproofing costs 2 to 4 times as much as interior waterproofing on the same basement, and understanding why — and what each approach actually does — is the key to not overpaying or underbuying. We cover this comparison in more depth in our guide to <a href="/interior-vs-exterior-basement-waterproofing/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-medium">interior vs. exterior basement waterproofing</a>, but here's the cost mechanics.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Exterior Costs So Much More</h3>
          <p>
            Exterior waterproofing means excavating the full perimeter of your foundation down to the footing — typically 6–8 feet deep — applying a waterproof membrane directly to the outside of the wall, installing new exterior drain tile at the footing, and backfilling. Every part of that process adds cost that interior work simply doesn't incur. Excavation alone requires heavy equipment (a mini-excavator or backhoe) and a crew for multiple days, running $8,000–$15,000 for an average home just to dig and backfill. Anything in the way — a deck, patio, mature landscaping, a driveway, or a fence — has to be removed and restored afterward, which is a separate cost most homeowners don't anticipate. And exterior work is entirely weather-dependent: a rainy week can shut down the job site for days, and no reputable contractor will apply a membrane to a damp wall or backfill over wet, unstable soil.
          </p>
          <p>
            Interior systems skip all of that. A crew works from inside the basement, breaking a narrow trench along the interior perimeter of the slab, installing drain tile in gravel, and routing the water to a sump pump — all in a controlled, weather-independent environment with no landscaping to disturb. That's the entire reason interior systems cost a fraction of exterior work: the labor and equipment burden is dramatically smaller.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Honest Tradeoff: What Interior Systems Don't Do</h3>
          <p>
            Here's the part contractors don't always spell out clearly. An interior drain tile and sump system does not stop water from entering the wall or slab — it never claims to. Water still penetrates the concrete exactly as it did before; the system simply intercepts that water at the interior perimeter, before it can spread across the floor, and redirects it to a sump basin and out of the house. The wall itself stays damp inside its own mass, and the moisture is still traveling through your foundation. For a basement used as unfinished storage space, that distinction rarely matters in practice. For a basement you plan to finish with framing and drywall directly against the foundation wall, it matters more, since trapped moisture behind interior framing can cause mold and rot even after a French drain has eliminated standing water.
          </p>
          <p>
            Exterior waterproofing, by contrast, stops water from reaching the wall at all — the membrane is a barrier on the soil side of the foundation, so the concrete stays dry rather than merely drained. That's a genuinely more complete fix, and it's the right call when you're dealing with structural repair, foundation crack sealing from the outside, or a basement you intend to finish with the wall assembly built directly against masonry. It's also often the more practical option if you're already excavating for an addition, egress window, or major landscaping project, since the marginal cost of adding waterproofing to an open excavation is much lower than starting from scratch.
          </p>

