                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">$140–$233/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">DIY Crack Injection</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$325</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10–15 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-green-700">$22–$33/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">DIY Interior French Drain + Sump</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,875</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15–20 years</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-green-700">$94–$125/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Interior French Drain + Sump</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$8,100</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25–30 years*</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">$270–$324/yr</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">Professional Exterior Excavation + Membrane</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$32,000</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40+ years*</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-900">$700–$800/yr</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 px-4 pb-4 pt-2">*Drain tile and membranes are long-lived, but the sump pump itself typically needs replacement every 7–10 years ($150–$600) regardless of system type — factor that into ongoing costs separately.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The numbers tell an honest, slightly uncomfortable story: even amortized over four decades, exterior excavation costs more per year than any interior option, because the upfront principal is so large. Interior systems remain the most cost-effective way to solve an ordinary wet basement, and DIY crack injection on an appropriate crack is the single best value in this entire guide. Exterior work earns its premium only when the wall itself needs to stay dry, not just drained — which is a real requirement for some basements and an unnecessary expense for most.
          </p>
          <p>
            The one case where interior systems look worse than the table suggests is a basement with severe, unaddressed hydrostatic pressure that overwhelms the drain tile's capacity — water bypassing the system and pooling anyway. If that happens, you're paying for both the interior system and, eventually, exterior work, which is the most expensive outcome of all. This is why an honest contractor assessment before you commit to a tier matters more than the price of any individual system. For general guidance on solving persistent water issues at any budget, see our roundup of <a href="/wet-basement-solutions/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-medium">wet basement solutions</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is interior or exterior basement waterproofing better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">It depends on what's causing the problem. Exterior waterproofing stops water before it ever reaches the foundation wall, which is the more complete fix, but it costs roughly 2 to 4 times as much because it requires full excavation, membrane installation, and landscaping restoration. Interior systems manage water after it has already entered the wall by redirecting it to a drain tile and sump pump — they don't stop moisture from penetrating the concrete itself, but they solve the practical problem of a wet basement floor for a fraction of the cost. For typical seepage and hydrostatic pressure issues, interior systems solve the everyday problem effectively. Exterior work earns its price when you have structural cracking, failing exterior walls, or you're already excavating for an addition or egress window.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a French drain system myself to save money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, and experienced DIYers do it, but it's one of the most physically demanding projects in home improvement — breaking out a strip of concrete floor with a jackhammer, hauling out the spoil, laying properly sloped perforated pipe in gravel, and correctly wiring a sump pump. Realistic savings run $5,000 to $9,500 versus a professional install on an average basement, but mistakes like incorrect pipe slope, insufficient gravel bed, or an undersized pump lead to failures that are expensive to fix once the concrete is patched back over. It's a reasonable project for a physically capable homeowner with help and rented equipment; it's not a good first DIY project if you're uncertain about basic plumbing or drainage principles.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does adding a battery backup sump pump cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">A battery backup sump pump system typically adds $400 to $1,000 to a basement waterproofing project — $200 to $600 for the backup unit and battery, plus $150 to $400 for installation if done alongside a primary pump. It's one of the highest-value add-ons available because the scenario it protects against — a power outage during a heavy storm — is exactly when your primary pump is most likely to be needed and most likely to fail without backup power.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a permit for basement waterproofing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700">It depends on your municipality and the scope of work. Interior drain tile and sump pump installations often don't require a building permit since they don't alter structural elements, though a plumbing permit may apply to the sump discharge line in some areas. Exterior excavation, foundation repair, and egress window additions almost always require a permit, typically $50 to $500 depending on locality, because they involve structural and drainage work that inspectors need to sign off on. Check with your local building department before starting either type of project — a licensed contractor should already be factoring this into their quote.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

