        <!-- CTA Block -->
        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Basement Waterproofing Budget Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Waterproofing quotes vary more than almost any other home repair, and the spread usually reflects genuinely different scopes of work rather than different margins.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Quotes That Solve Different Problems</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An interior drain tile system and an exterior excavation are not competing bids for the same job — they address water at different points and cost very differently. Get every contractor to state the diagnosis, not just the price, before you compare anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for Waterproofing Before Fixing Drainage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A surprising share of wet basements are caused by blocked gutters, short downspouts, or ground sloping back toward the house. Those fixes cost a few hundred dollars. Spending five figures on a system before ruling them out is the most expensive mistake in this category.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring What the Warranty Actually Covers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lifetime warranties in this industry frequently cover the installed system rather than the basement staying dry, exclude seepage through the walls, and lapse if the house changes hands. Read the transfer terms and exclusions — they explain a lot of the price difference between bids.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Out Restoration Costs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An interior system means breaking up the perimeter of the slab, and the quote often stops at concrete. Flooring, framing, drywall, and reinstating anything finished can add thousands. Ask what the finished state looks like on handover, and budget the gap.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Basement waterproofing costs</strong> range from a few hundred dollars for drainage corrections to well into five figures for exterior excavation, and the right number depends entirely on where the water is getting in. Diagnosis first, quotes second.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Start with the cheap external fixes — gutters, downspout extensions, and grading — and give them a full wet season to prove themselves. If water still comes in, get at least three quotes that each state the diagnosis, the scope, the warranty terms, and the restoration work included, and compare those rather than the headline prices.
          </p>
        </div>

